# Blobly Net project — J1939 demo (#171), zero drivers, no hardware.
#
# One CAN row, ticked J1939. Nothing here transmits: the demo is the RECORDING beside it.
#
#   BLOBLY_PROJECT=projects/j1939-demo.blobnet ./scripts/run_gui.sh
#   → Trace ▸ Open Recording ▸ samples/j1939-bam.log
#
# and the trace reads what a truck bus actually looks like, with no database loaded at all:
#
#   PGN F004 SA 0x00   and   PGN F004 SA 0x21     one parameter group, two senders
#   PGN EA00 SA 0x00 DA 0x03                      a request addressed to one node
#   the TP.CM/TP.DT frames of a multi-packet transfer, and the 20-byte message they
#   add up to, rejoined as a row of its own
#   an address claim, after which SA 0x21 is named rather than numbered
#
# Attach a DBC and the names replace `PGN ....`.
#
# WHY THE TICK, and why the recording needs none. A live wire has an owner to ask, so it is
# asked: no frame can say it is J1939, since every 29-bit identifier splits into a plausible
# group number and a plausible source address whether anybody meant it to or not. Most J1939
# databases declare nothing either — `VFrameFormat` is a Vector attribute they were written
# without — which is what this tick is for. A recording has nobody to ask, so it answers for
# itself: a well-formed transport announcement in the file is proof, and nothing else counts.
project:
  name: j1939-demo
  # v6, because `j1939:` below is a key a build that predates it drops on its next structured
  # save. An older version-aware build warns rather than silently rewriting the declaration
  # away (project.version_for; codex on #344).
  version: 6

channels:
  - name: J1939
    type: can
    interface: inproc:J1939   # driver-free; swap for `can0` or `pcan:PCAN_USBBUS1@250000`
    bitrate: 250000           # the J1939 rate; nothing in-process enforces it
    mode: normal
    enabled: true
    j1939: true
